-- title:
--   Principal congruences and the translation criterion for congruences of finite-signature algebras
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\mathbf A=(A,(f_i)_{i<k})$ is an algebra in the sense of the base definitions of this project: a carrier set $A$ (of arbitrary size) together with finitely many total operations $f_i:A^{n_i}\to A$, $i<k$, each of finite arity $n_i\ge 0$ (nullary operations are allowed). A *congruence* of $\mathbf A$ is an equivalence relation $\theta$ on $A$ that is compatible with every operation: if $a_j\,\theta\,b_j$ for every coordinate $j$, then $f_i(a_1,\dots,a_{n_i})\,\theta\,f_i(b_1,\dots,b_{n_i})$. Congruences are ordered by inclusion, and $\operatorname{Con}(\mathbf A)$ denotes this poset. For a tuple $d\in A^{n}$, a coordinate $j$ and $x\in A$, write $d[j\mapsto x]$ for the tuple obtained from $d$ by replacing its $j$-th entry with $x$.
--
--   This file introduces the following objects and records their basic properties.
--
--   1. **Equality congruence.** The identity relation $\Delta_A=\{(x,x):x\in A\}$ is a congruence of $\mathbf A$, and $x\,\Delta_A\,y$ holds exactly when $x=y$.
--   2. **Principal congruence.** For $a,b\in A$, the congruence generated by the pair $(a,b)$ is the intersection of all congruences that relate $a$ and $b$:
--   $$x\;\mathrm{Cg}(a,b)\;y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\text{for every }\theta\in\operatorname{Con}(\mathbf A):\ a\,\theta\,b\ \Longrightarrow\ x\,\theta\,y.$$
--   It is a congruence, it relates $a$ and $b$, and it is the least such congruence: for every $\theta\in\operatorname{Con}(\mathbf A)$,
--   $$\mathrm{Cg}(a,b)\subseteq\theta\iff a\,\theta\,b.$$
--   3. **Basic translations.** For an operation index $i<k$, a coordinate $j$ of $f_i$ and a tuple $d\in A^{n_i}$, the basic translation is the unary map
--   $$\tau_{i,j,d}:A\to A,\qquad \tau_{i,j,d}(x)=f_i\bigl(d[j\mapsto x]\bigr)=f_i(d_1,\dots,d_{j-1},x,d_{j+1},\dots,d_{n_i}),$$
--   obtained by fixing all inputs of $f_i$ except the $j$-th (the entry $d_j$ itself plays no role). Every congruence is preserved by every basic translation: $x\,\theta\,y$ implies $\tau_{i,j,d}(x)\,\theta\,\tau_{i,j,d}(y)$.
--   4. **Translation criterion.** An equivalence relation $\theta$ on $A$ is a congruence of $\mathbf A$ if and only if it is preserved by all basic translations $\tau_{i,j,d}$. This rests on a general fact about a single function: for any equivalence relation $\theta$ on a set $A$, any $n\ge 0$ and any $f:A^n\to A$ such that
--   $$x\,\theta\,y\ \Longrightarrow\ f\bigl(d[j\mapsto x]\bigr)\,\theta\,f\bigl(d[j\mapsto y]\bigr)\qquad\text{for all coordinates } j,\ d\in A^n,\ x,y\in A,$$
--   one has $f(a)\,\theta\,f(b)$ whenever $a_j\,\theta\,b_j$ for all $j$. (For $n=0$ there is nothing to check, since both input tuples are the empty tuple.)
--
--   The translation criterion reduces compatibility with operations of arbitrary finite arity to stability under unary maps, and principal congruences let one read a congruence off pair by pair: a congruence contains $\mathrm{Cg}(a,b)$ exactly when it relates $a$ and $b$. Both facts drive the graph characterization of finite congruence lattices, in which pairs of elements are colored by their principal congruences and the basic translations become label-decreasing self-maps.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `Congruence alg` is the subtype of `Setoid Carrier` cut out by `Compatible alg`, ordered as a subtype of setoids (that is, by inclusion) and coerced to a binary relation on the carrier; `ext`, `refl`, `symm`, `trans` and `compatible` are the extensionality and accessor lemmas. The definitions are `Congruence.equality` (the bottom setoid), `Congruence.principal` and `translation` (via `Function.update`); the lemmas are `equality_apply`, `principal_pair`, `principal_le`, `relation_map_of_updates` (the general fact in item 4), `translation_preserves` and `compatible_iff_translations`. Coordinates of an $n$-ary operation are indexed by `Fin n`.
